{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "57",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 70s with recurrent primary peritoneal carcinoma receiving pembrolizumab and ipilimumab presented with a pruritic rash ongoing for 2 weeks. The rash started as an erythematous papule on her right arm 2 months after initiation of a new immunotherapy regimen, then gradually enlarged and spread to involve both legs. She denied any new systemic symptoms associated with onset of the rash. A recent trial of oral terbinafine yielded no improvement.\n\nErythematous annular plaques without scale on her right arm and bilateral legs.\n\n- Biopsy findings:  - 4-mm punch biopsy from right arm showed superficial and deep perivascular and interstitial inflammation with numerous eosinophils  - Periodic acid–Schiff (PAS) stain was negative for fungal organisms- Images:  Image A Title: Erythematous annular plaques on left lower leg  Image A Description: Erythematous annular plaques without scale on the left lower leg    Image B Title: Erythematous annular plaques on right lower leg    Image B Description: Erythematous annular plaques without scale on the right lower leg    Image C Title: Histopathology - Inflammation pattern  Image C Description: Hematoxylin and eosin stain demonstrating superficial and deep perivascular and interstitial inflammation    Image D Title: Histopathology - Eosinophils  Image D Description: Hematoxylin and eosin stain demonstrating numerous scattered eosinophils",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Progressive Pruritic Rash with Perivascular Inflammation and Eosinophilia",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "pruritic rash ongoing for 2 weeks",
